Memoria — Privacy Policy
Last updated: August 27, 2026

The short version: Memoria does not collect, transmit, or sell any of your data.
Everything you keep stays on your device, and — only if you turn it on — in your
own private iCloud. Nothing is ever sent to the developer.

DEVELOPER
Memoria is developed by Dzianis Milasheuski, an individual developer based in
Lithuania. Questions about this policy: info@porconova.com

WHAT I COLLECT
Nothing. Memoria has no analytics, no advertising, no accounts, no feed, and no
servers that receive your data. There is no sign-up and no login. There are no
third-party SDKs and no trackers in the app. I do not know who you are, whom you
are remembering, or that you use the app at all.

The one exception is not mine to control and never contains your content: if you
have chosen in iOS Settings to share analytics and crash reports with app
developers, Apple may show me aggregated, anonymised statistics — how often the
app crashed, on which iOS versions. This comes from Apple, in aggregate, and
cannot be traced back to you or to anything you have kept. You can turn it off in
Settings › Privacy & Security › Analytics & Improvements.

WHAT THE APP HOLDS
Everything you put into Memoria is content you create: names, dates, written
memories, photographs, films, voice recordings, and the candles you light. It is
stored inside the app's private container on your device. Photographs, films and
recordings are kept as files in that container; the rest is kept in a local
database. None of it is indexed, counted, analysed, or shared.

Small previews of photographs and films are generated on your device with
Apple's own imaging frameworks and kept in the app's private cache. No image
leaves the device for this. The app stores no passwords and no credentials of
any kind — nothing in the Keychain, nothing anywhere else. When you switch on
Face ID, the check is performed by iOS and the app is told only whether it
passed.

PRIVATE iCLOUD SYNC (OPTIONAL, OFF BY DEFAULT)
If you turn on "Private iCloud sync" in Settings, Memoria stores a copy of your
people, days, words and media files in the PRIVATE database of your own iCloud
account, so your other devices signed in to the same Apple Account can show the
same place.
- The data goes only to your iCloud. It does not pass through any server of mine,
  and I have no access to it.
- Apple stores it under your Apple Account, governed by Apple's privacy policy.
- Turning sync off stops the copying; everything already on the device stays on
  the device.
- Settings › "Erase the iCloud copy" removes Memoria's records and files from
  your iCloud without touching anything on this device.
Sync also uses Apple's silent push notifications to learn that another device has
changed something. These carry no content of yours; they only wake the app.

PERMISSIONS THE APP MAY ASK FOR
- Photos — only to let you pick a photograph or a film to keep. Memoria reads
  what you pick, and nothing else in your library.
- Camera — only to photograph or film something at the moment you ask for it.
- Microphone — only to record a voice memory while you hold the recorder open.
- Face ID, Touch ID or your device passcode — to keep the app closed to anyone
  but you. Authentication happens inside iOS; the app receives only "yes" or
  "no" and never sees your biometric data or your passcode.
- Notifications — only for reminders you switch on yourself, for the days you
  have chosen. They are scheduled and delivered locally by your device. Memoria
  has no way to send you anything else, and no server that could.

WHAT LEAVES YOUR DEVICE
Only what you explicitly export or share, to the destination you pick. "Save a
copy of everything" produces a single file on your device which you may send
wherever you choose; where it goes afterwards is up to you. Nothing is uploaded
automatically, and nothing is ever sent to me.

That file is not encrypted and it holds everything — the names, the words, the
photographs, the films and the voices. Treat it exactly as you would treat the
originals: anyone who opens it sees all of it.

PURCHASES
Memoria Pro is sold through Apple's In-App Purchase. Apple, not the developer,
processes payment. I receive no personal or payment information. You can manage
or cancel a subscription in Settings › [Your Name] › Subscriptions on your
device.

YOUR RIGHTS
Because the app collects no personal information on the developer's side, there
is no developer-held data for you to access, correct, export, or delete.
- Anything kept in the app can be removed inside it, one item at a time, one
  person at a time, or all at once with Settings › "Delete everything".
- Everything can be exported at any time with Settings › "Save a copy of
  everything", in a format that can be opened again by the app.
- Data in your iCloud can be removed with Settings › "Erase the iCloud copy", by
  signing out of iCloud, or from your iCloud storage in iOS Settings.
- Deleting the app removes the on-device copy.
- An active subscription can be cancelled in your Apple Account subscriptions.

INFORMATION ABOUT OTHER PEOPLE
Memoria is a place to remember someone who has died, so what you keep will often
concern another person. That information is never transmitted to the developer or
to any third party. It lives on your device and, at your choice, in your own
iCloud, and it is yours to remove at any time.

CHILDREN
Memoria is not directed at children under the age of 13 and does not knowingly
collect any data from children.

REGULATORY NOTES
The app is designed so that no directly identifying personal information (name,
email, IP address, advertising identifier, account ID) is collected by the
developer or by any third party the developer uses. There are no third-party
SDKs, no trackers, and no data brokers. Where Apple acts as a processor for
purchases and for iCloud, those activities are governed by Apple's own privacy
policy. Because no personal data is collected or processed by the developer,
there is no data controller obligation to transfer data internationally, and no
data retention period to declare on the developer's side.

What you keep in Memoria will usually be personal data about another person —
someone who has died, and often other people in the photographs. It is created,
stored and erased entirely by you, on your own device and in your own iCloud;
the developer is neither its controller nor its processor and cannot reach it.
Keeping the memory of someone close is a purely personal and household activity,
which is outside the scope of the GDPR. If you were ever to use the app for
something other than that — in a professional or organisational context — you,
and not the developer, would be the controller of that data.

